Server-rendered lists become sortable through the `sortable-lists` Stimulus
controller suite. A drag or a move menu action sends one request describing
where the item landed; the server persists the order and streams the list back.

## Choosing an approach

**Prefer the declarative component API where it exists.** `BorderBoxListComponent`
is gaining `sortable_list:` / `sortable_item:` options backed by value objects
that emit this contract for you
([DREAM-805](https://community.openproject.org/wp/DREAM-805)). Once that lands
it covers the common case — a single list, or a set of sibling lists, of rows
the server can reorder — and you should not hand-wire anything.

Wire the controllers by hand only for what the component API does not reach:
multiple item types under one root, per-type move endpoints, non-`BorderBoxList`
markup such as table rows or cards, or optimistic placement.

**Do not use `generic-drag-and-drop`.** The dragula-based controller is
deprecated and closed to new consumers; its remaining call sites are being
migrated. Anything new uses `sortable-lists`.

Full API reference for the value objects will follow this page once the
component documentation tooling is in place; what follows is a usage guide for
manual wiring.

## Controller topology

Four controllers divide the work, connected to each other through outlets
rather than DOM traversal:

* **`sortable-lists`** — the root. Owns the move URLs, performs the request,
  and reconciles the result. Declares outlets for the three children.
* **`sortable-lists--list`** — one per list. Declares the list's own type, its
  id, and which item type it accepts. A list that accepts no type renders but
  refuses drops.
* **`sortable-lists--item`** — one per draggable row. Declares the item's id,
  type, and accessible label, and hosts the move menu actions.
* **`sortable-lists--scrollable`** — optional, on whatever element scrolls, to
  auto-scroll it while a drag hovers its edge.

The root scopes every child by outlet selector, so nested roots never accept
each other's items. Scope the selectors to the root's own wrapper id:

```ruby
{
  controller: "sortable-lists",
  sortable_lists_move_url_template_value: move_url_template,
  sortable_lists_sortable_lists__list_outlet: "##{wrapper_key} [data-controller~='sortable-lists--list']",
  sortable_lists_sortable_lists__item_outlet: "##{wrapper_key} [data-controller~='sortable-lists--item']"
}
```

Rows may sit in a child rows container — the list controller resolves
`:scope > ul`, as `BorderBoxList` renders — or directly under the list element
itself. Both work; no markup change is needed for table rows or cards.

## Wiring a list

`Admin::TextTransformActions` is the smallest complete reference
implementation. Its list declares the type it persists and the type it accepts:

```ruby
{
  controller: "sortable-lists--list",
  sortable_lists__list_type_value: AI::TextTransformAction::SORTABLE_LIST_TYPE,
  sortable_lists__list_accepted_type_value: AI::TextTransformAction::SORTABLE_LIST_TYPE,
  sortable_lists__list_name_value: t(".title")
}
```

`type` doubles as the persisted `list_type`, so a droppable list without one
cannot accept drops and warns in the console. `name` is announced to screen
readers on a cross-list move. Add `id` when several lists share a type — it
becomes the `list_id` the server receives. `drop_position` (`start` or `end`,
default `end`) decides where a drop onto empty space in the list lands.

Each row declares its own identity:

```ruby
{
  controller: "sortable-lists--item",
  sortable_lists__item_id_value: text_transform_action.id,
  sortable_lists__item_type_value: AI::TextTransformAction::SORTABLE_LIST_TYPE,
  sortable_lists__item_label_value: text_transform_action.label
}
```

Set `confined: true` on an item the server will reorder in place but refuses to
relocate: it stays fully draggable, and its own list still accepts it, so
within-list reorder keeps working while foreign lists refuse the drop.

## The move URL

`move_url_template` is a URI template expanded client-side with the dragged
item's id. Build it from the route helper via a sentinel so installations under
a relative URL root keep working:

```ruby
def move_url_template
  id_placeholder = "__id__"
  drop_admin_text_transform_action_path(id_placeholder).sub(id_placeholder, "{id}")
end
```

The expansion must stay same-origin and relative — only path, query and
fragment survive. Where one root holds several item types, supply
`move_url_templates_value` as a type-keyed object instead; the dragged item's
type selects the endpoint, and `move_url_template` remains the fallback.

## The move request

The root sends a `PUT` to the expanded URL with `responseKind: 'turbo-stream'`
and three form fields:

| Field | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `list_type` | the destination list's `type` |
| `list_id` | the destination list's `id`, or blank when it declares none |
| `prev_id` | id of the row the item now follows, or blank when it landed first |

`prev_id` is an anchor, not an index, so a stale ordinal cannot silently move
the wrong row. The `Lists::MoveAfterAnchor` concern implements the server half
for `acts_as_list` models: a blank `prev_id` moves to the top, and an anchor
that is unknown, outside the given scope, or the record itself is refused
without mutating anything.

Answer a refusal with `422`; the client keeps that status silent apart from the
rollback, because the server has already streamed its own error message. Any
other failure raises a generic toast.

A drop that leaves the row where it started sends no request at all.

## Response policy

The row moves in the DOM immediately, before the request resolves. What
happens next is the consumer's choice:

* **Morph reconciliation (default).** The endpoint streams the re-rendered
  list, and the morph replaces the optimistic placement with the canonical
  server order. Use this unless you have a reason not to.
* **Event-only (`optimistic`).** Setting the root's `optimistic` value appends
  `optimistic=true` to the move URL. The endpoint may then acknowledge with an
  event alone and leave the client's placement standing. Only surfaces that
  cannot afford a full re-render on every drag opt in; Backlogs is the only one
  today.

  The flag is a request, not a promise. Backlogs skips the re-render only for a
  same-list move whose requested anchor is verifiably the persisted one — a
  blank `prev_id` with nothing above the row, or a non-blank one that really is
  the row above. A cross-list move, or an anchor that has meanwhile left the
  list, falls back to reloading the frame, because the persisted order and the
  optimistic order have diverged.

Either way a failed request rolls the rows back to their pre-move positions —
unless a concurrent morph has already moved them, in which case the fresher
server state wins.

## Move menus

Drag is a pointer gesture, so every sortable surface also needs keyboard- and
screen-reader-reachable move actions. Put them in the row's Primer
`ActionMenu`, with the data on the item level so Primer renders it on the
`<li>` the item controller listens to:

```ruby
menu.with_item(
  label: I18n.t(label),
  tag: :button,
  data: {
    sortable_lists__item_target: "moveItem",
    sortable_lists__item_direction_param: direction,
    action: "click->sortable-lists--item#move"
  }
) do |item|
  item.with_leading_visual_icon(icon:)
end
```

Availability is recomputed each time the menu opens, since an earlier reorder
may have shifted the row's neighbours. Unavailable directions are hidden by
default; set the item's `hide_unavailable` value to `false` to disable them
instead.

## Auto-scrolling

Put `sortable-lists--scrollable` on the element that actually scrolls — often
an ancestor of the root rather than the list. It defaults to the vertical axis
at standard speed; `allowed_axis` (`vertical`, `horizontal`, `all`) and
`max_scroll_speed` (`standard`, `fast`) adjust that.

## Batch moves

Multi-select and multi-row drags are in active development
([AGILE-278](https://community.openproject.org/wp/AGILE-278),
[AGILE-361](https://community.openproject.org/wp/AGILE-361)). The root's
`selection_enabled` value and the collection move
endpoint it drives are not yet a stable contract; this section will document
them once they settle.

## Migrating off generic-drag-and-drop

| `generic-drag-and-drop` | `sortable-lists` |
| --- | --- |
| `generic_drag_and_drop_target: "container"` | a `sortable-lists--list` controller on the list |
| `generic_drag_and_drop_target: "scrollContainer"` | a `sortable-lists--scrollable` controller |
| `drop-url` on the row | `move_url_template` on the root |
| `draggable-type` / `target-allowed-drag-type` | the item's `type` and the list's `accepted_type` |
| index-based position params | `list_type` + `list_id` + `prev_id` |
| no keyboard path | `moveItem` menu targets |

The endpoint changes shape as well: dragula posted an index, so a server that
still computes positions from one needs porting to anchor semantics before its
list can move over.
